WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.800 --> 00:00:05.360
We started with a question:

2
00:00:05.360 --> 00:00:07.700
what are microservices actually for?

3
00:00:07.800 --> 00:00:09.620
We end with a way to govern them.

4
00:00:09.620 --> 00:00:11.680
Let's assemble the whole picture.

5
00:00:12.330 --> 00:00:13.620
Here's the runtime.

6
00:00:13.620 --> 00:00:16.060
A request becomes a ChenileExchange,

7
00:00:16.060 --> 00:00:18.630
runs through the interception pipeline

8
00:00:18.630 --> 00:00:19.650
— your policies

9
00:00:19.650 --> 00:00:23.420
— reaches pure business logic, and travels back out.

10
00:00:23.520 --> 00:00:27.190
The same model runs in the gateway and in every service.

11
00:00:27.190 --> 00:00:29.290
That single fact lets one policy

12
00:00:29.290 --> 00:00:32.820
abstraction serve both the edge and the last mile.

13
00:00:33.470 --> 00:00:34.760
Chenile is layered.

14
00:00:34.760 --> 00:00:37.010
chenile-parent sets the versions.

15
00:00:37.010 --> 00:00:38.910
chenile-core is the runtime.

16
00:00:38.910 --> 00:00:39.930
Then blueprints

17
00:00:39.930 --> 00:00:42.830
— for queries, workflows and processes.

18
00:00:42.930 --> 00:00:44.130
Then integration:

19
00:00:44.130 --> 00:00:47.370
the registry, proxies, security and messaging.

20
00:00:47.370 --> 00:00:51.140
And finally testing, auxiliary tools, and cconfig.

21
00:00:51.790 --> 00:00:53.150
Eleven repositories.

22
00:00:53.150 --> 00:00:54.620
One release train.

23
00:00:55.270 --> 00:00:57.140
Five moves, in one breath.

24
00:00:57.240 --> 00:00:57.500
One:

25
00:00:57.500 --> 00:01:00.840
adopt services, for deployability and ownership.

26
00:01:00.940 --> 00:01:01.170
Two:

27
00:01:01.170 --> 00:01:03.980
split the definition from the implementation.

28
00:01:04.080 --> 00:01:04.470
Three:

29
00:01:04.470 --> 00:01:07.250
turn horizontal concerns into policies.

30
00:01:07.350 --> 00:01:07.640
Four:

31
00:01:07.640 --> 00:01:10.860
place policies at the gateway, and at the last mile.

32
00:01:10.960 --> 00:01:11.270
Five:

33
00:01:11.270 --> 00:01:12.990
use one abstraction for both

34
00:01:12.990 --> 00:01:15.630
— with the registry choosing placement.

35
00:01:16.280 --> 00:01:18.840
The same discipline reaches everywhere.

36
00:01:18.840 --> 00:01:23.020
One Gherkin spec, run as a unit test or an integration test.

37
00:01:23.120 --> 00:01:27.690
One pub-sub interface over MQTT, Kafka, Azure or in-JVM

38
00:01:27.690 --> 00:01:31.430
— every message through the same pipeline.

39
00:01:31.530 --> 00:01:33.270
Configuration with cconfig

40
00:01:33.270 --> 00:01:36.360
— modular, per tenant, overridable at runtime.

41
00:01:36.360 --> 00:01:40.110
And proxies that call any service through its interface,

42
00:01:40.110 --> 00:01:42.570
with interception on the way out.

43
00:01:43.220 --> 00:01:45.040
Business logic stays pure.

44
00:01:45.040 --> 00:01:47.000
Governance stays consistent.

45
00:01:47.000 --> 00:01:48.820
Placement is configuration

46
00:01:48.820 --> 00:01:50.190
— not a rewrite.

47
00:01:50.840 --> 00:01:52.660
Generate your first service

48
00:01:52.660 --> 00:01:55.030
— episode four shows every command.

49
00:01:55.030 --> 00:01:58.660
Read the story on chenile.org, and star the repos.

50
00:01:58.760 --> 00:01:59.190
Chenile:

51
00:01:59.190 --> 00:02:01.920
separate what a service does from how it's governed

52
00:02:01.920 --> 00:02:03.300
— everywhere it runs.
